#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167837} :activity/id #uuid "1ac0408b-df85-5aed-a27c-f13d969faefe" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049396 :topic/id #uuid "f141a839-e982-56b6-ad89-068e132234e3" :topic/math-academy-id 3188} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167837 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167994 :tutorial/content "Consider a system of two differential equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\boldsymbol{f}(x(t),y(t))\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/70/705a93663ca9c14ad66b98356a6f705179af4acb04759feb766b07e673facfcb.svg) and ![](images/61/612b78f653c4fa85bd90f69eb240a8fb3bf89e2c548517045833d73bef7488c7.svg) is a vector-valued function of $x(t)$ and $y(t).$\n\nIn this context, the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the **phase plane** (or **phase space**) of the system.\nAt each time $t,$ the solution has a position\n![](images/d0/d029e673d68410790a0ef1b11971ef894ba3980bae4ff62f701b5db605375e84.svg)\nwhich we view as a point $(x(t),y(t))$ in the phase plane. Now, as $t$ changes, the point $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ moves and traces out a curve in the phase plane.\nThis curve is called the **trajectory** (or **orbit**) of the solution.\n\nFor instance, consider the following (linear) system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-4x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}=x(t)-5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA few trajectories (particular solutions) of the system are shown below.\n\n![](images/fe/fe17a02809800422e4f5cfa0d6a906a57ce1b9de06344092a28decea37e81e23.png)\n\nNotice that the derivative $\\mathbf{x}'(t)$ can be interpreted as the *tangent vector* to the trajectory at the point $\\mathbf{x}(t).$\nIn the diagram above, the (normalized) tangents to the trajectories are shown as small gray vectors. \n\nAt each point in the phase plane, the system assigns a unique tangent vector. Taken together, these tangent vectors form the system’s **vector field**.\n\nIn our example above, suppose a trajectory (solution) passes through the point $(x,y)=(1,-2).$ Then, substituting the coordinates of this point into the equations of our system, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =-4x+2y \\\\  & =-4(1)+2(-2) \\\\  & =-8 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =x-5y \\\\  & =(1)-5(-2) \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent vector to the trajectory at $(x,y)=(1,-2)$ is\n![](images/1e/1e67b9a345556d4aaad1044ad7ba66ddeac33765834556b33e325b20aa686b3c.svg)\n\nLet's see some more examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "0956c86b-1feb-53d8-b7bb-330bfed8dbe8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14424 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8292d50f-e642-5a75-8162-58bfebcc96f3" :step/math-academy-id 587425 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167838}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167838 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166909 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166533 :question/id #uuid "86054cff-8fae-561d-a57e-31e573a45a91" :question/math-academy-id "e-21585"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74b8d3a6-0298-5f65-ad28-e2fc3cde731d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050098}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390992}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent to a Trajectory Given an Initial Value Problem"} :step/id #uuid "4b97f9ac-3366-56b8-9ed6-d50ce4187ff6" :step/math-academy-id 587426 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167839}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167839 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167995 :tutorial/content "Again, consider an autonomous system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=g(x(t),y(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor which at a point $(x,y)$ in the phase plane, the tangent vector is\n![](images/ec/ec1021a54eaaaefba4051abfc1fc91b34a223c1d4f3e21deb36326a0495ced14.svg)\n\nWe can now define two special sets of points in the phase space:\n\n- The **$x$-nullcline** is the set of points where $x'=0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=0.\n$$\n\nOn the $x$-nullcline, the tangent vector has the form\n![](images/43/439174cae9cf07578504710223cdcf391d73917eb6a8f68be6294811b197d164.svg)\nso the tangents to trajectories are *vertical*.\n\n- The **$y$-nullcline** is the set of points where $y'=0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=0.\n$$\n\nOn the $y$-nullcline, the tangent vector has the form\n![](images/1d/1dee443cccfac50017c010235bc39e748da8c382a809d635042f53b127f4cda8.svg)\nso the tangents to trajectories are *horizontal*.\n\nFor example, in the linear case, if the system is given as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(t)+by(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}=cx(t)+dy(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the nullclines are the straight lines with equations\n\n$$\nax+by=0\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ncx+dy=0.\n$$\n\nLet's see some concrete examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "f4abda1f-5bb9-5eb6-ae6f-f7c48b003f00" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14425 :tutorial/title "Nullclines"} :step/id #uuid "2ebe76d2-d337-56e3-a6bc-fb01fee75628" :step/math-academy-id 587429 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167840}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167840 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166910 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166534 :question/id #uuid "63995ae4-21fa-5777-85ca-93585258b9f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-21586"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d219270-5202-54e6-8cba-dc816155bd7b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391032}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Nullclines of a System of Differential Equation"} :step/id #uuid "fcbf4721-736d-5084-908d-f66341d4a8e7" :step/math-academy-id 587430 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167841}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167841 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167996 :tutorial/content "A **phase portrait** is a diagram that shows the trajectories of a system along with their tangent directions.\n\nPhase portraits are usually generated with software. However, for simple linear systems, we can often sketch them by hand using the procedure below.\n\n- Start at a point $(x_0,y_0).$\n\n- Draw a short line segment in the direction indicated by the direction field at that point. To determine the direction, we can use the vector field (if given) or compute the tangent vector at $(x_0,y_0),$ as we did earlier.\n\n- Move a small step in the direction we just drew.\n\nRepeat Steps 2 and 3, taking small steps and adjusting the direction each time to match the direction field at the new point.\n\nAs we sketch, we can use *nullclines* to help determine where the trajectories must turn:\n\n- On the $x$-nullcline (where $x'=0$), the trajectory is vertical.\n\n- On the $y$-nullcline (where $y'=0$), the trajectory is horizontal.\n\nFinally, remember that trajectories do not form sharp corners: they bend smoothly as the tangent direction changes.\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "54cb4976-e5e4-5f8d-beaf-ad953f0e39bc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14427 :tutorial/title "Sketching Trajectories"} :step/id #uuid "be653111-d5ba-5c6f-a527-5eaa83ff2692" :step/math-academy-id 587433 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167842}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167842 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166911 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166535 :question/id #uuid "e0888bda-120f-52bb-a6f9-c5af9737139f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21588"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "129bee33-7c18-5450-affe-dd710a3a75dd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048917}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391124}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Trajectory Given a Starting Point and the Vector Field"} :step/id #uuid "6b56fb31-a151-5c54-ae42-48e825d9d00b" :step/math-academy-id 587434}] :activity/title "Phase Planes and Phase Portraits" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166986}]}
